// The relaxed half of the listing10/listing11 pair: a read of a location the
// thread has just written may be forwarded, and the dependency then goes.
//
// Source: the MRD line's numbered out-of-thin-air listings. Both files date
// from the first litmus commit (8aa5b1a) and neither assertion has ever been
// changed -- only the syntax was normalised. listing11.lit is the same program
// with volatile accesses, asserting the opposite, and the two differ in nothing
// else. That is the pair: (2,3) is the read-to-write dependency, and whether it
// can be dropped is what volatility decides.
//
// It was parked twice over. c1cdb61 merged it back to
// litmus-tests/avoidoota/litmus-tests-review/avoidoota/listing10.lit -- a
// review path nested inside the scanned tree -- and 0bca217 read the mangled
// path as a stray copy of listing11 rather than the original file it is.
// Under it, `notin` required both events to be in the execution, and the read
// is elided by forwarding in exactly the execution that witnesses the
// assertion, so the observation was answered false (#42).
x := 0;
r1 := x;
y := r1;
%% allow ((2,3) ∉ .dp) []
